1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,953
Spring, 1220.

2
00:00:03,173 --> 00:00:05,392
The richest city on the Silk Road

3
00:00:05,392 --> 00:00:07,179
is behind eight iron gates,

4
00:00:07,179 --> 00:00:09,615
with tens of thousands of soldiers on

5
00:00:09,615 --> 00:00:10,319
its walls.

6
00:00:10,789 --> 00:00:12,731
Outside, an army arrives.

7
00:00:12,951 --> 00:00:15,122
In front of it marches a crowd

8
00:00:15,122 --> 00:00:17,636
so large that the defenders think it

9
00:00:17,636 --> 00:00:19,351
is a second Mongol army.

10
00:00:19,821 --> 00:00:20,951
It is not.

11
00:00:21,171 --> 00:00:22,515
They are prisoners.

12
00:00:22,735 --> 00:00:24,380
Captured men from Bukhara,

13
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:26,767
carrying banners, pushed to the front.

14
00:00:27,237 --> 00:00:29,392
Behind them rides Genghis Khan.

15
00:00:29,612 --> 00:00:31,575
This is the siege of Samarkand,

16
00:00:31,575 --> 00:00:33,021
and by the end of it,

17
00:00:33,021 --> 00:00:34,519
an empire will be gone.

18
00:00:34,739 --> 00:00:37,115
If you like history told with real

19
00:00:37,115 --> 00:00:38,643
sources, tap like now.

20
00:00:38,863 --> 00:00:40,612
It really helps the channel.

21
00:00:43,282 --> 00:00:46,226
To understand Samarkand, go back two years.

22
00:00:46,446 --> 00:00:50,543
In 1218, the Khwarazmian Empire was the

23
00:00:50,543 --> 00:00:53,507
great power of Central Asia.

24
00:00:53,727 --> 00:00:57,404
It ruled from today's Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan

25
00:00:57,404 --> 00:00:59,274
to Iran and Afghanistan.

26
00:00:59,744 --> 00:01:02,240
Its ruler was Shah Muhammad the Second.

27
00:01:02,460 --> 00:01:04,396
To the east, a new power had

28
00:01:04,396 --> 00:01:05,740
risen on the steppe:

29
00:01:05,740 --> 00:01:07,623
Genghis Khan and the Mongols.

30
00:01:08,093 --> 00:01:10,079
At the border town of Otrar,

31
00:01:10,079 --> 00:01:12,918
the governor seized a Mongol trade caravan

32
00:01:12,918 --> 00:01:14,621
and killed its merchants.

33
00:01:14,841 --> 00:01:17,404
When Genghis sent envoys to ask for

34
00:01:17,404 --> 00:01:20,504
justice, the shah's men killed or humiliated

35
00:01:20,504 --> 00:01:21,219
them too.

36
00:01:21,689 --> 00:01:23,826
There is a famous story that the

37
00:01:23,826 --> 00:01:26,603
Mongols later killed the governor by pouring

38
00:01:26,603 --> 00:01:29,007
molten silver into his eyes and ears.

39
00:01:29,227 --> 00:01:30,728
It makes a great scene,

40
00:01:30,728 --> 00:01:32,747
but historians call it a legend.

41
00:01:33,217 --> 00:01:34,988
What is not a legend is what

42
00:01:34,988 --> 00:01:35,627
came next.

43
00:01:35,847 --> 00:01:40,250
In late 1219, the Mongol armies crossed

44
00:01:40,250 --> 00:01:41,842
into Khwarazm.

45
00:01:44,512 --> 00:01:46,496
How big was the Mongol army?

46
00:01:46,716 --> 00:01:49,868
Medieval Muslim writers gave four hundred to

47
00:01:49,868 --> 00:01:51,687
six hundred thousand men.

48
00:01:51,907 --> 00:01:54,168
Modern historians call that absurd.

49
00:01:54,638 --> 00:01:57,492
Today, most estimates for the whole invasion

50
00:01:57,492 --> 00:01:59,962
run from about ninety thousand to two

51
00:01:59,962 --> 00:02:01,059
hundred thousand.

52
00:02:01,279 --> 00:02:03,336
Some put the upper limit near one

53
00:02:03,336 --> 00:02:04,991
hundred and twenty thousand.

54
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:06,592
And the shah?

55
00:02:06,812 --> 00:02:09,289
Some accounts give him far more soldiers

56
00:02:09,289 --> 00:02:10,631
than the Mongols had.

57
00:02:10,851 --> 00:02:12,365
So why did he lose?

58
00:02:12,835 --> 00:02:15,264
Because Muhammad did not trust his own

59
00:02:15,264 --> 00:02:15,950
commanders.

60
00:02:16,170 --> 00:02:18,551
Instead of meeting the Mongols in one

61
00:02:18,551 --> 00:02:20,879
big battle, he spread his troops out

62
00:02:20,879 --> 00:02:22,308
into the great cities,

63
00:02:22,308 --> 00:02:24,213
and waited behind their walls.

64
00:02:26,883 --> 00:02:28,948
That let Genghis take them one by

65
00:02:28,948 --> 00:02:29,251
one.

66
00:02:29,471 --> 00:02:31,711
Two of his sons besieged Otrar.

67
00:02:31,931 --> 00:02:34,213
Other columns went north and south.

68
00:02:34,433 --> 00:02:37,209
And Genghis himself, with his youngest son

69
00:02:37,209 --> 00:02:39,319
Tolui, vanished into the desert.

70
00:02:39,789 --> 00:02:41,819
He crossed the Kyzylkum,

71
00:02:41,819 --> 00:02:44,830
about three hundred miles of desert,

72
00:02:44,830 --> 00:02:46,861
and appeared at Bukhara,

73
00:02:46,861 --> 00:02:49,242
far behind the shah's lines,

74
00:02:49,242 --> 00:02:51,693
on the 7th of February 1220.

75
00:02:52,163 --> 00:02:54,957
Bukhara's outer city gave up within days.

76
00:02:55,177 --> 00:02:57,717
Its citadel held for about twelve days,

77
00:02:57,717 --> 00:02:58,420
then fell.

78
00:02:58,640 --> 00:03:00,688
Everyone inside was killed.

79
00:03:01,158 --> 00:03:03,624
One account says Genghis rode into the

80
00:03:03,624 --> 00:03:05,714
main mosque and told the people:

81
00:03:05,714 --> 00:03:07,537
I am the punishment of God.

82
00:03:07,757 --> 00:03:09,975
If you had not committed great sins,

83
00:03:09,975 --> 00:03:12,194
God would not have sent a punishment

84
00:03:12,194 --> 00:03:13,303
like me upon you.

85
00:03:13,773 --> 00:03:15,075
The city burned.

86
00:03:15,295 --> 00:03:17,065
The great mosque was destroyed.

87
00:03:17,285 --> 00:03:19,623
But one tower, the Kalyan minaret,

88
00:03:19,623 --> 00:03:20,251
survived.

89
00:03:20,471 --> 00:03:22,418
You can still see it in Bukhara

90
00:03:22,418 --> 00:03:22,817
today.

91
00:03:23,287 --> 00:03:25,675
The craftsmen of Bukhara were sent to

92
00:03:25,675 --> 00:03:27,533
make weapons for the Mongols.

93
00:03:27,753 --> 00:03:29,417
The young men were taken too.

94
00:03:29,637 --> 00:03:31,255
And they were marched east,

95
00:03:31,255 --> 00:03:32,431
to the next target.

96
00:03:32,901 --> 00:03:34,949
So here is my question for you.

97
00:03:35,169 --> 00:03:36,477
If you were the shah,

98
00:03:36,477 --> 00:03:38,657
would you have risked one big battle,

99
00:03:38,657 --> 00:03:40,691
or would you also have trusted the

100
00:03:40,691 --> 00:03:41,079
walls?

101
00:03:41,299 --> 00:03:42,749
Tell me in the comments.

102
00:03:45,419 --> 00:03:50,198
In March 1220, the Mongols reached Samarkand.

103
00:03:50,418 --> 00:03:52,401
They came along both banks of the

104
00:03:52,401 --> 00:03:53,319
Zarafshan River.

105
00:03:53,789 --> 00:03:55,112
The city was ready.

106
00:03:55,332 --> 00:03:58,412
Medieval writers say it held thirty thousand

107
00:03:58,412 --> 00:04:01,491
Turkic soldiers, fifty thousand local troops,

108
00:04:01,491 --> 00:04:03,268
and twenty war elephants.

109
00:04:03,488 --> 00:04:06,197
Another estimate says a hundred thousand.

110
00:04:06,667 --> 00:04:09,588
Modern historians think these numbers are too

111
00:04:09,588 --> 00:04:09,974
high.

112
00:04:10,194 --> 00:04:13,082
Chroniclers of this period often exaggerated,

113
00:04:13,082 --> 00:04:15,249
so treat every number here as a

114
00:04:15,249 --> 00:04:16,082
rough guess.

115
00:04:16,552 --> 00:04:18,419
Then came the trick from the start

116
00:04:18,419 --> 00:04:19,219
of this video.

117
00:04:19,439 --> 00:04:22,337
Genghis put the Bukhara prisoners in front,

118
00:04:22,337 --> 00:04:25,121
holding banners, so his army looked twice

119
00:04:25,121 --> 00:04:25,689
as big.

120
00:04:25,909 --> 00:04:28,155
They were also sent first against the

121
00:04:28,155 --> 00:04:28,555
walls.

122
00:04:28,775 --> 00:04:30,396
Many of them died there.

123
00:04:30,866 --> 00:04:32,722
The defenders tried to break out.

124
00:04:32,942 --> 00:04:35,397
Cavalry and the war elephants charged out

125
00:04:35,397 --> 00:04:36,249
of the gates.

126
00:04:36,719 --> 00:04:38,873
The Mongols did what they did best.

127
00:04:39,093 --> 00:04:41,234
They pulled back as if running away,

128
00:04:41,234 --> 00:04:43,424
then turned and trapped the attackers.

129
00:04:43,894 --> 00:04:45,622
How many died in that ambush?

130
00:04:45,842 --> 00:04:48,189
One source says about a thousand.

131
00:04:48,409 --> 00:04:50,329
Another says fifty thousand.

132
00:04:50,549 --> 00:04:52,959
That is how uncertain these numbers are.

133
00:04:53,429 --> 00:04:55,752
The captured elephants were let loose in

134
00:04:55,752 --> 00:04:56,672
the countryside.

135
00:04:56,892 --> 00:04:58,712
Without anyone to feed them,

136
00:04:58,712 --> 00:04:59,729
they soon died.

137
00:05:02,399 --> 00:05:04,682
Inside the city, people lost hope.

138
00:05:04,902 --> 00:05:06,678
The shah did not come to save

139
00:05:06,678 --> 00:05:07,014
them.

140
00:05:07,234 --> 00:05:08,863
According to one account,

141
00:05:08,863 --> 00:05:11,090
he did try to send cavalry twice,

142
00:05:11,090 --> 00:05:12,610
but then he fled west.

143
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:15,647
So the Muslim clergy of Samarkand urged

144
00:05:15,647 --> 00:05:17,176
the people to give up.

145
00:05:17,396 --> 00:05:18,868
The gates were opened.

146
00:05:19,338 --> 00:05:22,241
Most of the Turkic garrison surrendered too,

147
00:05:22,241 --> 00:05:23,748
on the 16th of March,

148
00:05:23,748 --> 00:05:25,311
according to one count.

149
00:05:25,531 --> 00:05:27,857
Another chronicle gives the 19th.

150
00:05:28,077 --> 00:05:29,933
They hoped to join the Mongols.

151
00:05:30,153 --> 00:05:32,179
Genghis accepted, and waited.

152
00:05:34,849 --> 00:05:36,961
About two thousand men refused.

153
00:05:37,181 --> 00:05:39,390
They locked themselves in the citadel and

154
00:05:39,390 --> 00:05:39,976
fought on.

155
00:05:40,446 --> 00:05:42,392
How long they held is disputed,

156
00:05:42,392 --> 00:05:44,491
from less than two weeks to about

157
00:05:44,491 --> 00:05:45,054
a month.

158
00:05:45,274 --> 00:05:47,385
In the end, around a thousand of

159
00:05:47,385 --> 00:05:49,655
them broke out at night and escaped

160
00:05:49,655 --> 00:05:51,397
south across the Amu Darya.

161
00:05:51,617 --> 00:05:52,961
The rest were killed.

162
00:05:53,431 --> 00:05:55,618
Then Genghis turned on the soldiers who

163
00:05:55,618 --> 00:05:56,503
had surrendered.

164
00:05:56,723 --> 00:05:58,557
He had them executed.

165
00:05:58,777 --> 00:06:01,273
One count says about three thousand men.

166
00:06:01,493 --> 00:06:03,456
Another says thirty thousand.

167
00:06:03,926 --> 00:06:06,315
Samarkand was looted from end to end.

168
00:06:08,985 --> 00:06:11,057
You may hear that the Mongols killed

169
00:06:11,057 --> 00:06:13,081
two and a half million people here.

170
00:06:13,301 --> 00:06:16,113
That number comes from the Persian historian

171
00:06:16,113 --> 00:06:18,600
Juvaini, and it actually refers to the

172
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:20,384
sieges of Urgench and Merv.

173
00:06:20,604 --> 00:06:22,737
Historians say it is impossible.

174
00:06:22,957 --> 00:06:24,957
The whole empire may not have had

175
00:06:24,957 --> 00:06:27,053
much more than three million people.

176
00:06:27,523 --> 00:06:29,123
The truth is bad enough.

177
00:06:29,343 --> 00:06:32,538
Bukhara, Samarkand, Urgench and Merv were all

178
00:06:32,538 --> 00:06:34,226
taken in about a year,

179
00:06:34,226 --> 00:06:35,914
and each was hit hard.

180
00:06:36,384 --> 00:06:37,600
And Muhammad?

181
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:40,308
Genghis sent his two best generals,

182
00:06:40,308 --> 00:06:42,321
Jebe and Subutai, after him.

183
00:06:42,541 --> 00:06:44,804
The shah ran from town to town,

184
00:06:44,804 --> 00:06:46,254
and died soon after,

185
00:06:46,254 --> 00:06:48,400
a hunted man, on an island in

186
00:06:48,400 --> 00:06:49,560
the Caspian Sea.

187
00:06:50,030 --> 00:06:51,573
His son, Jalal al-Din,

188
00:06:51,573 --> 00:06:53,230
kept fighting for years.

189
00:06:53,450 --> 00:06:56,266
But the Khwarazmian Empire was finished.

190
00:06:58,936 --> 00:07:00,685
So what changed on the map?

191
00:07:00,905 --> 00:07:05,105
Before 1220, Central Asia belonged to the

192
00:07:05,105 --> 00:07:05,705
shah.

193
00:07:05,925 --> 00:07:09,443
After 1220, it belonged to the Mongol

194
00:07:09,443 --> 00:07:13,350
Empire, and soon to Genghis's son Chagatai

195
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:14,757
and his heirs.

196
00:07:15,227 --> 00:07:16,955
Samarkand did not die.

197
00:07:17,175 --> 00:07:19,818
Genghis left an official named Yelü Ahai

198
00:07:19,818 --> 00:07:20,589
to run it.

199
00:07:20,809 --> 00:07:23,718
Within a year, travelers from China reported

200
00:07:23,718 --> 00:07:26,291
Chinese and Khitan craftsmen moving in.

201
00:07:26,761 --> 00:07:29,628
But the city's walls stayed in ruins

202
00:07:29,628 --> 00:07:32,039
for a hundred and fifty years,

203
00:07:32,039 --> 00:07:35,297
until 1370, when Amir Temur made Samarkand

204
00:07:35,297 --> 00:07:37,577
his capital and rebuilt them.

205
00:07:38,047 --> 00:07:41,536
Today, Samarkand is the second-largest city of

206
00:07:41,536 --> 00:07:44,959
Uzbekistan, and a UNESCO World Heritage Site.

207
00:07:45,179 --> 00:07:47,688
Bukhara and its old minaret are in

208
00:07:47,688 --> 00:07:48,763
Uzbekistan too.

209
00:07:48,983 --> 00:07:50,932
Otrar, where it all began,

210
00:07:50,932 --> 00:07:53,002
is now a field of ruins in

211
00:07:53,002 --> 00:07:54,402
southern Kazakhstan.

212
00:07:54,872 --> 00:07:56,941
Three facts most people never hear.

213
00:07:57,161 --> 00:07:59,273
Samarkand had eight iron gates.

214
00:07:59,493 --> 00:08:01,925
Its defenders fought with war elephants.

215
00:08:02,145 --> 00:08:04,595
And the Mongols used their own prisoners

216
00:08:04,595 --> 00:08:05,921
to make a fake army.

217
00:08:06,391 --> 00:08:08,944
So, was Samarkand lost on its walls,

218
00:08:08,944 --> 00:08:11,265
or lost the day the shah decided

219
00:08:11,265 --> 00:08:12,599
to split his army?

220
00:08:12,819 --> 00:08:14,414
Tell me what you think in the

221
00:08:14,414 --> 00:08:14,889
comments.

222
00:08:15,109 --> 00:08:18,237
And subscribe, because next time we go

223
00:08:18,237 --> 00:08:19,801
to Russia in 1380,

224
00:08:19,801 --> 00:08:21,910
to the field of Kulikovo,

225
00:08:21,910 --> 00:08:25,242
where Moscow first beat the Golden Horde.

